WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.019
Metacognition makes learning more active.

2
00:00:02.019 --> 00:00:10.097
Instead of passively consuming content, the learner plans practice, monitors progress, uses retrieval, spaces review, seeks feedback, and adjusts methods.

3
00:00:10.097 --> 00:00:17.367
The learner asks what capability is being built and what evidence would show that the capability is real.

4
00:00:17.367 --> 00:00:21.002
This keeps learning connected to performance instead of completion.

5
00:00:21.002 --> 00:00:25.041
Planning practice means choosing the skill and the exercise deliberately.

6
00:00:25.041 --> 00:00:31.100
If the goal is better troubleshooting, practice may focus on hypothesis formation or evidence collection.

7
00:00:31.100 --> 00:00:36.754
If the goal is better writing, practice may focus on outlining or revising claims.

8
00:00:36.754 --> 00:00:43.217
If the goal is better communication, practice may focus on summarizing uncertainty or asking clearer questions.

9
00:00:43.217 --> 00:00:47.256
Monitoring progress means noticing whether the current method is working.

10
00:00:47.256 --> 00:00:48.467
Are errors repeating?

11
00:00:48.467 --> 00:00:51.698
Is the learner retrieving the idea without looking?

12
00:00:51.698 --> 00:00:55.737
Is the task too easy, too hard, or productively challenging?

13
00:00:55.737 --> 00:00:57.757
Has feedback identified a bottleneck?

14
00:00:57.757 --> 00:01:06.642
A metacognitive learner adjusts difficulty and strategy instead of assuming that more time with the same method will automatically solve the problem.

15
00:01:06.642 --> 00:01:10.681
This also helps learners know when to ask for help.

16
00:01:10.681 --> 00:01:18.759
Asking for help is more effective when the learner can name the target, the attempt, the gap, and the uncertainty.

17
00:01:18.759 --> 00:01:29.664
Instead of saying, I do not get it, the learner can say, I can follow the example, but I cannot choose the method on a new problem.

18
00:01:29.664 --> 00:01:33.703
That is a much better starting point for useful feedback.
